  "query_preview": "range, some of the 00Z/06Z CAMs show little to no \nactivity in Kansas Saturday morning given a possible lack of \nconvection in Nebraska Friday night. So, there is a chance no \nmuch of anything occurs Saturday morning. Regardless, the \nsynoptic frontal boundary will be stalled out across northern \nKansas on Saturday, and a few showers and storms are possible \nduring peak afternoon heating along it. The main concern with \nshowers and storms along I-70 Saturday afternoon will be the \npotential for downbursts producing damaging wind gusts. Shower \nand thunderstorm activity could linger through much of Saturday \nnight into Sunday morning due in part to a subtle shortwave \ntrough embedded in zonal flow aloft traversing eastward over the\ncentral plains. The frontal boundary is forecast to wash out by\nSunday morning, and another humid day with afternoon highs in \nthe 90s is expected for Sunday.\n\n     BEGINNING OF NEXT WEEK...\n\nA more robust trough is forecast to dig into the Rockies and Great \nPlains by the beginning of the work week next week which could \npotentially lead to another period of active weather. It's a bit too \nearly to get into the finer details at time; however, it appears \nincreasingly probable that a cold front will stall out across Kansas \nMonday and Tuesday leading to potentially several rounds of showers \nand storms. Heavy amounts of rainfall don't appear to be on the \ntable, but should this play out as currently forecast, it would \nprovide meaningful rainfall to areas that need it. In addition to \nthe potential for rainfall, much cooler temperatures are also \nforecast in the wake of the frontal passage with afternoon highs \npotentially in the 70s! Temperatures will recover slightly by mid-\nweek, but will be near, if not slightly below, average for this\ntime of year.",
